{"data": [{"author_id": "1445660613879812100", "created_at": "2026-11-04T08:23:06.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117895397473480885"], "id": "2117895397473480885", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 1, "impression_count": 355, "like_count": 14, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 3, "retweet_count": 1}, "text": "⚛️ THE QUANTUM FRONTIER #008\n\nWhat happens when a quantum computer makes a mistake? 🤔\n\nUnlike ordinary computers, quantum computers are extremely sensitive to noise.\n\nHeat, vibrations and imperfect operations can introduce errors.\n\nThat's where FAULT TOLERANCE comes in!\n\nTHE", "url": "https://x.com/TheFrankzy/status/2117895397473480885"}, {"author_id": "1445660613879812100", "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:23:56.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118348595292160048"], "id": "2118348595292160048", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 1, "impression_count": 263, "like_count": 6, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 5, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "⚛️ THE QUANTUM FRONTIER #009\n\n🐰 Why aren't more qubits always better?\n\nImagine building a computer with 1,000 qubits.\n\nSounds impressive, right?\n\nBut there's a problem.\n\nQuantum information is extremely fragile.\n\nHeat, noise and imperfect operations can introduce errors.\n\nThat's", "url": "https://x.com/TheFrankzy/status/2118348595292160048"}, {"author_id": "1489260991321759747", "created_at": "2026-11-04T12:58:27.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117964692694188231"], "id": "2117964692694188231", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 2, "impression_count": 2227, "like_count": 21, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 2, "retweet_count": 1}, "text": "Oh dear, @grok. You've confused demonstrating error suppression in an encoded quantum memory with demonstrating a fully operational, fault-tolerant logical qubit capable of performing the required computations.\n\nWillow's celebrated result concerned quantum memory and repeated https://t.co/79bISmVUMB", "entities": {"mentions": [{"end": 14, "id": "1720665183188922368", "start": 9, "username": "grok"}], "urls": [{"display_url": "x.com/grok/status/21…", "end": 300, "expanded_url": "https://twitter.com/grok/status/2117963880227840172", "start": 277, "url": "https://t.co/79bISmVUMB"}]}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2117963880227840172", "type": "quoted"}], "url": "https://x.com/Dr_CSWright/status/2117964692694188231"}, {"author_id": "836979680951533569", "created_at": "2026-11-05T05:07:41.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118208607166865478"], "id": "2118208607166865478", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 2, "impression_count": 320, "like_count": 8, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 1, "retweet_count": 3}, "text": "Frequency Wave Theory: Solving the Quantum Computing Revolution\n\nQuantum computers promise breakthroughs in medicine, ASi, energy, and materials science, but there's a fundamental problem: we still haven't built one capable of delivering broad, practical advantages over the best https://t.co/npZZAJKQJo https://t.co/ah4Nlen9R0", "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/npZZAJKQJo", "end": 303, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/drew_ponder/status/2118208607166865478/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118208593426309120", "start": 280, "url": "https://t.co/npZZAJKQJo"}, {"display_url": "x.com/skdh/status/21…", "end": 327, "expanded_url": "https://twitter.com/skdh/status/2117661155900764301", "start": 304, "url": "https://t.co/ah4Nlen9R0"}]}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2117661155900764301", "type": "quoted"}], "url": "https://x.com/drew_ponder/status/2118208607166865478"}, {"author_id": "2116987497611681792", "created_at": "2026-11-05T00:34:50.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118139943675388388"], "id": "2118139943675388388", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 12, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 1, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "Photonic quantum computing hits a manufacturing problem before a qubit problem.\n\nQuiX Quantum is in P4Q, a €50M EU pilot line for quantum photonic chips: 29 partners, 12 countries, through March 2029.\n\nQuiX plans logical-qubit demos for 2028 to 2030.\n\nCan the fab keep pace? https://t.co/wglDA1n43E", "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/wglDA1n43E", "end": 298, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/QuantumDevIO/status/2118139943675388388/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118139911840649216", "start": 275, "url": "https://t.co/wglDA1n43E"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/QuantumDevIO/status/2118139943675388388"}, {"author_id": "1894294404325937152", "created_at": "2026-11-05T18:20:36.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118408153603977278"], "id": "2118408153603977278", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 5, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "A quantum computer won't replace your laptop — but Google's Willow proved one can fix its own errors: 101 qubits guarding one logical qubit that errs just 0.143% of the time. What quantum can (and can't) do in 2026:\nhttps://t.co/dULadTOVZP\n#QuantumComputing #Tech", "entities": {"hashtags": [{"end": 257, "start": 240, "tag": "QuantumComputing"}, {"end": 263, "start": 258, "tag": "Tech"}], "urls": [{"description": "Latest science and technology news explained clearly, from research breakthroughs to the tech shaping everyday life.", "display_url": "lk-99kor.blogspot.com/2026/11/quantu…", "end": 239, "expanded_url": "https://lk-99kor.blogspot.com/2026/11/quantum-computing-explained-what-it-can.html", "images": [{"height": 630, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2118408155898253312/Y6JVWjqS?format=jpg&name=orig", "width": 1200}, {"height": 150, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2118408155898253312/Y6JVWjqS?format=jpg&name=150x150", "width": 150}], "start": 216, "status": 200, "title": "Quantum Computing Explained: What It Can Actually Do in 2026 — and What Is Still Hype | LK99", "unwound_url": "https://lk-99kor.blogspot.com/2026/11/quantum-computing-explained-what-it-can.html", "url": "https://t.co/dULadTOVZP"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/kimstorm1980/status/2118408153603977278"}, {"author_id": "2110525628084547584", "created_at": "2026-11-03T22:00:02.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117738599667917114"], "id": "2117738599667917114", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 16, "like_count": 1, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "Quantum explainer: a physical qubit is one fragile piece of hardware. A logical qubit spreads its info across many physical qubits so errors can be caught and fixed. Breaking today's crypto needs many reliable logical qubits. Track readiness: https://t.co/d6FGlWSk8y #Science", "entities": {"hashtags": [{"end": 275, "start": 267, "tag": "Science"}], "urls": [{"description": "Drag and click glass helium bubbles for the top 100 crypto tokens, in a stone observatory open to the Milky Way. Each bubble fills with green as its privacy and quantum upgrade progress grows.", "display_url": "quantumhelium.pages.dev", "end": 266, "expanded_url": "https://quantumhelium.pages.dev/", "images": [{"height": 630, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2117738653359206400/bVKmOWZF?format=jpg&name=orig", "width": 1200}, {"height": 150, "url": "https://pbs.twimg.com/news_img/2117738653359206400/bVKmOWZF?format=jpg&name=150x150", "width": 150}], "start": 243, "status": 200, "title": "Quantum Helium Maps · StarSwarm Agentic Security", "unwound_url": "https://quantumhelium.pages.dev/", "url": "https://t.co/d6FGlWSk8y"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/starswarm/status/2117738599667917114"}, {"author_id": "830872631918354433", "created_at": "2026-11-04T22:25:03.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118107284173955322"], "id": "2118107284173955322", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 30, "like_count": 4, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 1}, "text": "The quantum race has shifted from qubit counts to logical qubits and verifiable results. IBM’s public target is demonstrated quantum advantage by the end of 2026. Google has already published a verifiable-advantage result on Willow &amp; Quantinuum is pushing logical-qubit execution.", "url": "https://x.com/bitsofexpertise/status/2118107284173955322"}, {"author_id": "1692398763396997120", "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:20:46.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118045808092385472"], "id": "2118045808092385472", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 16, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 2, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "Monit Sharma preprint: LM-assisted circuit search cuts the payoff-arithmetic Toffoli x logical-qubit product 2.39x and 4.84x on two finance benchmarks. Catch: 0 tolerance violations in 1M random paths, but 241 of 900 picked stress paths failed. Random tests aren't proof.", "url": "https://x.com/AmericaGetBig/status/2118045808092385472"}, {"author_id": "1783980123613478914", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:19:04.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118332268749943013"], "id": "2118332268749943013", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 59, "like_count": 1, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "$AMD x $IBM quantum partnership is worth understanding.\n\n- IBM's 120-qubit Nighthawk processor pairs with AMD's Umbra supercomputer for real-time error correction\n- As quantum systems scale, classical computing demand scales with them — not against them\n- AMD doesn't need to", "entities": {"cashtags": [{"end": 4, "start": 0, "tag": "AMD"}, {"end": 11, "start": 7, "tag": "IBM"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/NGLSHMUELI/status/2118332268749943013"}, {"author_id": "1627439165246783489", "created_at": "2026-11-05T15:21:36.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118363106237677860"], "id": "2118363106237677860", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 81, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "$AMD + $IBM — Quantum Error Correction Platform\n\nOak Ridge National Lab, IBM, and AMD launched a co-design platform that pairs IBM’s 120-qubit Nighthawk processor with ORNL’s Umbra supercomputer (powered by AMD Instinct MI355X GPUs and EPYC CPUs) to develop and test quantum error https://t.co/s5IcCdqUdN", "entities": {"cashtags": [{"end": 4, "start": 0, "tag": "AMD"}, {"end": 11, "start": 7, "tag": "IBM"}], "urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/s5IcCdqUdN", "end": 304, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/financespotnews/status/2118363106237677860/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118363098868326401", "start": 281, "url": "https://t.co/s5IcCdqUdN"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/financespotnews/status/2118363106237677860"}, {"author_id": "3932993782", "created_at": "2026-11-04T00:09:02.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2117771064801386583"], "id": "2117771064801386583", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 2113, "like_count": 13, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 6}, "text": "Foxconn Wants To Build The Quantum Computers.\n\nFoxconn's chairman, Young Liu, told CNBC the company wants to become the world's largest quantum computer manufacturer, without owning the underlying technology.\n\nThe plan is to build machines for the companies that develop it. Liu https://t.co/9sRTAOhEnf", "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/9sRTAOhEnf", "end": 302, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/Quan_Chain/status/2117771064801386583/video/1", "media_key": "13_2117771040768036864", "start": 279, "url": "https://t.co/9sRTAOhEnf"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/Quan_Chain/status/2117771064801386583"}, {"author_id": "1718295299989409792", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:01:28.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118327841536307399"], "id": "2118327841536307399", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 62, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "$IBM | Launches Quantum Error Correction Co-Design Platform With ORNL and AMD\nType: Partnership &amp; Contract Win | Materiality: Low | Sentiment: Neutral 🟠\n\nIBM, Oak Ridge National Laboratory's Quantum Science Center and AMD unveiled a co-design platform for quantum error", "entities": {"cashtags": [{"end": 4, "start": 0, "tag": "IBM"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/underthechart/status/2118327841536307399"}, {"author_id": "18638888", "created_at": "2026-11-05T15:51:33.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118370644400222244"], "id": "2118370644400222244", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 40, "like_count": 2, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "***NEWS***: Every practical quantum computer will need a powerful classical computer working beside it - and @AMD is building that foundation now. 🚀 \n\nWith @IBM and @ORNL, we've launched a new co-design platform for quantum error correction that pairs IBM's 120-qubit Nighthawk https://t.co/TTtiCTmgWe", "entities": {"mentions": [{"end": 113, "id": "14861876", "start": 109, "username": "AMD"}, {"end": 160, "id": "18994444", "start": 156, "username": "IBM"}, {"end": 170, "id": "16145097", "start": 165, "username": "ORNL"}], "urls": [{"display_url": "x.com/AMD/status/211…", "end": 301, "expanded_url": "https://twitter.com/AMD/status/2118330823803851060", "start": 278, "url": "https://t.co/TTtiCTmgWe"}]}, "referenced_tweets": [{"id": "2118330823803851060", "type": "quoted"}], "url": "https://x.com/jbgeorge/status/2118370644400222244"}, {"author_id": "1306359926164516864", "created_at": "2026-11-05T16:47:46.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118384792777654756"], "id": "2118384792777654756", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 3, "impression_count": 10259, "like_count": 19, "quote_count": 1, "reply_count": 9, "retweet_count": 3}, "text": "INFLEQTION SAYS IT WILL HAVE A TRULY USEFUL QUANTUM COMPUTER BY 2028\n\nQuantum computers are built from qubits. Most qubits make a lot of mistakes. To fix that, companies group several of them together into one reliable \"logical qubit.\" Logical qubits are the ones that actually https://t.co/3i7WLxZNVU", "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/3i7WLxZNVU", "end": 301, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/WOLF_Financial/status/2118384792777654756/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118384667820740608", "start": 278, "url": "https://t.co/3i7WLxZNVU"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/WOLF_Financial/status/2118384792777654756"}, {"author_id": "1413458321319727115", "created_at": "2026-11-05T15:25:08.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118363996889706964"], "id": "2118363996889706964", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 751, "like_count": 10, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 1}, "text": "IonQ's 2030 target is 2 million qubits.\n\nThat's not the number that matters.\n\nThis one does: ~80,000.\n\nThose are logical qubits — the error-corrected, stable ones that actually run algorithms. Physical qubits are noisy, so many get bundled into each reliable logical qubit.\n\nMuch", "url": "https://x.com/quantum_nyang/status/2118363996889706964"}, {"author_id": "1808835479611265025", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:26:06.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118334040738599067"], "id": "2118334040738599067", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 156, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "IBM and AMD have launched a platform pairing IBM’s 120-qubit Nighthawk processor with AMD’s Umbra supercomputer to test quantum error correction in real time. The move underscores how scaling quantum systems raises demand for classical compute to curb errors.", "url": "https://x.com/TU_Crypto_News/status/2118334040738599067"}, {"author_id": "2103861891978399744", "created_at": "2026-11-05T15:13:51.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118361154661789953"], "id": "2118361154661789953", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 7, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 1, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "AMD, IBM and Oak Ridge just launched a co-design platform to fix quantum error correction. IBM's 120-qubit Nighthawk meets AMD's Umbra supercomputer. Source in reply. https://t.co/kSq1m4lMSO", "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/kSq1m4lMSO", "end": 190, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/WarisHymii/status/2118361154661789953/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118361085762019329", "start": 167, "url": "https://t.co/kSq1m4lMSO"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/WarisHymii/status/2118361154661789953"}, {"author_id": "1212958909", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:18:05.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118332021110141097"], "id": "2118332021110141097", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 25, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "IBM, Oak Ridge National Laboratory (ORNL), and AMD have jointly launched a new co-design platform for quantum error correction. The platform pairs IBM’s most powerful quantum processor — the 120-qubit IBM Quantum Nighthawk — with ORNL’s Umbra high-performance computing system, https://t.co/C9TkPAJLf6", "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/C9TkPAJLf6", "end": 301, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/Dragon21Soul/status/2118332021110141097/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118331995063496704", "start": 278, "url": "https://t.co/C9TkPAJLf6"}, {"display_url": "pic.x.com/C9TkPAJLf6", "end": 301, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/Dragon21Soul/status/2118332021110141097/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118332012188794880", "start": 278, "url": "https://t.co/C9TkPAJLf6"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/Dragon21Soul/status/2118332021110141097"}, {"author_id": "2116618276507693056", "created_at": "2026-11-05T14:48:22.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118354741537227218"], "id": "2118354741537227218", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 0, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "IBM, AMD and Oak Ridge have launched a quantum error correction platform pairing the 120-qubit Nighthawk with AMD-powered Umbra, the companies said. IBM estimates the field needs a 230-fold gain. https://t.co/9FqOKsSwG2", "entities": {"urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/9FqOKsSwG2", "end": 219, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/voltgridnews/status/2118354741537227218/photo/1", "media_key": "3_2118354652362145792", "start": 196, "url": "https://t.co/9FqOKsSwG2"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/voltgridnews/status/2118354741537227218"}, {"author_id": "1722596454156443648", "created_at": "2026-11-05T18:04:23.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118404073783451669"], "id": "2118404073783451669", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 7, "like_count": 0, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "IBM, Oak Ridge and AMD launched a testbed pairing IBM's 120-qubit Nighthawk with AMD-powered supercomputing for quantum error correction.\nWhy: a classical decoder must catch errors in real time. IBM so far: 438 hard ops; DOE's 2028 goal: 100,000. @IBMResearch @AMD #Quantum https://t.co/DDq0Xy8oUR", "entities": {"hashtags": [{"end": 273, "start": 265, "tag": "Quantum"}], "mentions": [{"end": 259, "id": "16319797", "start": 247, "username": "IBMResearch"}, {"end": 264, "id": "14861876", "start": 260, "username": "AMD"}], "urls": [{"display_url": "pic.x.com/DDq0Xy8oUR", "end": 297, "expanded_url": "https://x.com/QuanCPT/status/2118404073783451669/video/1", "media_key": "13_2118404035002671104", "start": 274, "url": "https://t.co/DDq0Xy8oUR"}]}, "url": "https://x.com/QuanCPT/status/2118404073783451669"}, {"author_id": "846008252437475328", "created_at": "2026-11-05T13:40:54.000Z", "edit_history_tweet_ids": ["2118337763942785289"], "id": "2118337763942785289", "public_metrics": {"bookmark_count": 0, "impression_count": 80, "like_count": 1, "quote_count": 0, "reply_count": 0, "retweet_count": 0}, "text": "$AMD and $IBM\njust launched a platform pairing IBM's 120-qubit Nighthawk processor with AMD's Umbra supercomputer to test quantum error correctio